If $0$ of one unit means $0$ of the other, the line passes through the origin and its equation is $y = kx$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[step=1,gray!25,very thin] (0,0) grid (4.8,4.8);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(5.3,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,5.3) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,blue] (0,0)--(4.6,4.14);
\fill (0,0) circle (2.5pt);
\node[fill=white,inner sep=1pt] at (1.6,4.5) {$y=kx$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

If $0$ of one unit does not mean $0$ of the other, the line misses the origin and its equation is $y = kx + c$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[step=1,gray!25,very thin] (0,0) grid (4.8,4.8);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(5.3,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,5.3) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,blue] (0,1.4)--(4.6,4.8);
\fill (0,1.4) circle (2.5pt);
\node[left] at (0,1.4) {$c$};
\fill (0,0) circle (2.5pt);
\node[below left] at (0,0) {$O$};
\node[fill=white,inner sep=1pt] at (1.7,4.4) {$y=kx+c$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

To convert with the graph, trace from the known axis across to the line, then to the other axis; no equation is needed.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[step=1,gray!25,very thin] (0,0) grid (4.8,4.8);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(5.3,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,5.3) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,blue] (0,0)--(4.6,3.45);
\draw[dashed,red,->] (3.2,0)--(3.2,2.4);
\draw[dashed,red,->] (3.2,2.4)--(0,2.4);
\fill (3.2,2.4) circle (2.5pt);
